[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Postman Monitors and Scheduled Runs"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Automated API testing is crucial for ensuring the continuous health and reliability of your backend services. Postman Monitors allow you to run collections on a scheduled basis in the cloud, alerting your team immediately when tests fail."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites for Setting Up a Monitor"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A Postman account with an active workspace and access to Postman Cloud."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A collection containing requests with written test scripts (using pm.test()) to validate responses."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"An environment file if your requests rely on dynamic variables or specific base URLs."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Preparing Your Collection for Monitoring"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before creating a monitor, ensure your collection handles state and assertions properly. Monitors run headless in the cloud, meaning console logs should be used sparingly and tests must explicitly assert expected outcomes."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"pm.test("Status code is 200", function () {
    pm.response.to.have.status(200);
});

pm.test("Response time is less than 500ms", function () {
    pm.expect(pm.response.responseTime).to.be.below(500);
});"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Creating a Monitor"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a monitor in Postman, navigate to your workspace, select the collection you wish to monitor, click the three dots (...) next to the collection name, and select "Monitor collection". Alternatively, you can go to the "Monitors" tab in the sidebar and click "Create Monitor"."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Configuring Schedule and Regions"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Configure the operational parameters for your monitor. You can specify a name, select the environment to use during the run, choose the polling frequency (e.g., every hour, daily, or weekly), and select the geographic regions from which the requests will originate."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Frequency: Determines how often Postman executes the scheduled run."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Regions: Allows testing latency and availability from multiple global AWS regions."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Email Notifications: Configure who receives alerts when a monitor run fails."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Integrating with Slack or Webhooks"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"To keep your engineering team informed, you can route monitor results to external communication channels. Postman natively supports integrations with Slack, Microsoft Teams, Datadog, PagerDuty, and custom Webhooks."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"{"text": "Postman Monitor failure detected in collection: Authentication API"}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Postman Monitors"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep monitor runs lightweight to avoid consuming excessive Postman execution limits."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use environment variables to handle tokens that expire, utilizing pre-request scripts to fetch new tokens if necessary."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Review the monitor dashboard regularly to identify intermittent latency spikes."}]
